---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { CONTACT } from '../../data/site';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Someone has died', url: '/someone-has-died/' },
  { name: 'Registering a death', url: '/someone-has-died/registering-a-death/' },
];

const faq = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Where can I register a death in East Lothian?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'East Lothian Council registrations are handled at John Muir House, Brewery Park, Haddington EH41 3HA, by appointment. You may also register at any other registration office in Scotland — it does not have to be the district where the person lived or died.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How long do I have to register a death in Scotland?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Eight days from the date of death. This is longer than in England and Wales, where the limit is five days.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Does it cost anything to register a death?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Registering is free. There is a fee for each extra death certificate, which you will usually need several of for banks, pensions and insurers.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Page
  title="Registering a Death in East Lothian — What to Do"
  heading="Registering a death in East Lothian"
  description="How to register a death in East Lothian: where to go, what to take, how long you have, and what Tell Us Once actually covers."
  standfirst="You have eight days in Scotland, and you can register at any office in the country. Here is what the appointment actually involves."
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  schema={faq}
>
  <Callout tone="accent" title="The thing most people do not know">
    <p>
      In Scotland you may register a death at <strong>any</strong> registration office in
      the country. It does not have to be where the person died, or where they lived. If
      it is easier to register near your own home than to travel back to East Lothian,
      you can.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Where to go in East Lothian</h2>

  <p>
    East Lothian Council's registration service operates from:
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>John Muir House</strong><br />
    Brewery Park, Haddington, EH41 3HA<br />
    Telephone: <a href="tel:+441620827308">01620 827308</a><br />
    Email: <a href="mailto:haddingtonregistrars@eastlothian.gov.uk">haddingtonregistrars@eastlothian.gov.uk</a>
  </p>

  <p>
    Appointments are required — you cannot turn up. Book by telephone or through the
    council's online booking service. Appointments are usually available within two or
    three days, so the eight-day limit is rarely a problem.
  </p>

  <Callout title="If you live in the Borders">
    <p>
      Families in Berwickshire register with Scottish Borders Council instead. We have
      listed the offices across the region, with contact details and booking
      arrangements, in our{' '}
      <a href="/directory/registrars/">registrar directory</a>.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>How long you have</h2>
  <p>
    Eight days from the date of death. That is longer than in England and Wales, where
    the limit is five. If the death has been reported to the Procurator Fiscal, the clock
    effectively pauses until the Fiscal releases it — you will not be penalised for a
    delay that is not yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can register</h2>
  <p>Any of the following, in rough order of who is usually asked:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A relative of the person who died</li>
    <li>Someone who was present at the death</li>
    <li>The executor, or the person's solicitor</li>
    <li>The occupier of the building where the death happened</li>
    <li>Anyone else who knows the details and is accepting responsibility for the funeral</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to take with you</h2>
  <p>
    The Medical Certificate of Cause of Death is usually sent to the registrar
    electronically now, so you may not need to carry anything. Take what you can find of
    the following — the registrar will work with whatever you have:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>The person's birth certificate</li>
    <li>Their marriage or civil partnership certificate</li>
    <li>Their National Insurance number</li>
    <li>Their NHS medical card, if there is one</li>
    <li>Any pension or benefit details</li>
    <li>Your own photo identification</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you cannot find some of it, go anyway. Missing paperwork almost never stops a
    registration.
  </p>

  <h2>What the registrar will ask</h2>
  <p>
    The full name and any previous names, date and place of birth, occupation, usual
    address, marital status, and the names and occupations of the person's parents —
    including their mother's maiden surname. This last one catches people out, so it is
    worth asking a relative before you go.
  </p>

  <h2>What you come away with</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The Certificate of Registration of Death (Form 14).</strong> This is what we
      need before the funeral can go ahead. It is free.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An abbreviated extract of the death entry.</strong> Also free.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Full extracts — the "death certificates".</strong> These are chargeable, and
      you will need one for each bank, pension provider, insurer and share registrar.
      Most families need somewhere between four and eight. Buying them at the appointment
      is cheaper than ordering them later.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tell Us Once</h2>
  <p>
    The registrar will offer you the Tell Us Once service. It notifies HMRC, the
    Department for Work and Pensions, the Passport Office, DVLA, and East Lothian
    Council's own services in a single step, so you do not have to write the same letter
    a dozen times. It is worth the few extra minutes at the appointment.
  </p>
  <p>
    It does not cover banks, private pensions, insurers or utility companies — those you
    still have to contact yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do</h2>
  <p>
    We cannot register the death for you — that has to be someone connected to the person
    who died. But we will tell you when the certificate has reached the registrar, help
    you work out how many extracts to buy, and take care of everything that follows from
    it. If you are unsure about any of it, telephone us on{' '}
    <a href={CONTACT.phoneHref} class="font-semibold">{CONTACT.phone}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/arranging-a-funeral/">Arranging the funeral</a></li>
    <li><a href="/directory/registrars/">Registration offices across the east of Scotland</a></li>
    <li><a href="/funeral-costs/help-with-funeral-costs/">Help with funeral costs</a></li>
  </ul>
</Page>
